1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,009
Namaste. This is Liam, in for Bear, with a short film for INFO 7375, Computational

2
00:00:05,029 --> 00:00:10,709
Skepticism for AI. You might think a good test is one that passes. But a pass only

3
00:00:10,729 --> 00:00:13,391
means something if the test could have failed.

4
00:00:13,461 --> 00:00:17,838
Three terms. Falsifiable: some possible result could prove it wrong. Fail condition:

5
00:00:17,858 --> 00:00:22,602
the result that counts as failure, written down before you look. And risky

6
00:00:22,622 --> 00:00:25,167
prediction: a forecast the world could contradict.

7
00:00:25,237 --> 00:00:29,681
The move comes from Karl Popper, in The Logic of Scientific Discovery, first

8
00:00:29,701 --> 00:00:34,145
published in German in nineteen thirty-four. A claim is scientific only if some

9
00:00:34,165 --> 00:00:39,295
possible result could prove it wrong. In his words: it must be possible for an

10
00:00:39,315 --> 00:00:42,042
empirical scientific system to be refuted by experience.

11
00:00:42,112 --> 00:00:46,905
Popper noticed theories in his day that could explain any case at all. Whatever

12
00:00:46,925 --> 00:00:51,030
happened, it counted as a confirmation. Their admirers called that strength. Popper

13
00:00:51,050 --> 00:00:55,842
decided it was their weakness: a theory that no possible event could refute isn't

14
00:00:55,862 --> 00:00:56,186
scientific.

15
00:00:56,256 --> 00:01:00,664
Einstein's theory was different. It said starlight passing the sun would bend, by a

16
00:01:00,684 --> 00:01:04,775
definite amount. The eclipse expeditions of nineteen nineteen set out to tell three

17
00:01:04,795 --> 00:01:09,202
answers apart: no shift, a small shift, or Einstein's larger one. They reported his.

18
00:01:09,222 --> 00:01:13,630
But what impressed Popper was the risk: had the shift been clearly absent, the

19
00:01:13,650 --> 00:01:14,895
theory was simply refuted.

20
00:01:14,965 --> 00:01:19,384
Now a test in code. This one checks only that the function returned something. Feed

21
00:01:19,404 --> 00:01:23,824
it working code: it passes. Feed it broken code: it passes. A test that always

22
00:01:23,844 --> 00:01:27,375
passes isn't testing anything. It can't tell you which one you had.

23
00:01:27,445 --> 00:01:31,936
Rewrite it with a fail condition, written first: for this input, the answer must

24
00:01:31,956 --> 00:01:36,448
match the known result, or the test fails. Popper said it plainly: criteria of

25
00:01:36,468 --> 00:01:41,282
refutation have to be laid down beforehand. Now broken code fails, and a pass means

26
00:01:41,302 --> 00:01:41,604
something.

27
00:01:41,674 --> 00:01:45,845
Claims work the same way. The system is robust. What result would count against

28
00:01:45,865 --> 00:01:50,036
that? None is named, so it can't fail. Popper called vagueness like that a

29
00:01:50,056 --> 00:01:53,628
soothsayer's trick. Rewrite it: error rate on unfamiliar inputs stays under two

30
00:01:53,648 --> 00:01:57,220
percent, over any thirty days. Now a bad month proves it wrong.

31
00:01:57,290 --> 00:02:01,897
Agents make claims too: done, success. Picture an agent that grades its own work. It

32
00:02:01,917 --> 00:02:06,216
stacks the blocks: success. It knocks them over: success. It does nothing at all:

33
00:02:06,236 --> 00:02:10,534
success. An agent that succeeds no matter what it does isn't succeeding. It's just

34
00:02:10,554 --> 00:02:11,151
doing stuff.

35
00:02:11,221 --> 00:02:16,146
This happens with real models. Anthropic's system card for Claude 3.7 Sonnet reports

36
00:02:16,166 --> 00:02:19,949
that, after repeated failures, it occasionally special-cased tests in agentic

37
00:02:19,969 --> 00:02:25,275
coding: it returned the exact values the tests expected, or edited the tests to

38
00:02:25,295 --> 00:02:29,839
match its code. The tests passed. The general solution never got written.

39
00:02:29,909 --> 00:02:33,952
So write down, before anything runs, the result that would count as failure, and

40
00:02:33,972 --> 00:02:38,596
check it from outside the agent, not in its own report. If we don't design things

41
00:02:38,616 --> 00:02:42,370
beforehand so they could be wrong, we're asserting they'll always work, no matter

42
00:02:42,390 --> 00:02:42,660
what.

43
00:02:42,730 --> 00:02:46,827
Your turn. Take one claim, test, or agent success check you rely on, and paste this

44
00:02:46,847 --> 00:02:50,945
into Claude: Here is a claim, test, or agent success check I rely on: paste it.

45
00:02:50,965 --> 00:02:55,320
Before I run it, help me write the specific result that would prove it wrong: what I

46
00:02:55,340 --> 00:02:58,922
would observe, how I would measure it, and the threshold. If no possible result

47
00:02:58,942 --> 00:03:03,040
could prove it wrong, say so, and rewrite it so one could. Then check two things

48
00:03:03,060 --> 00:03:06,643
yourself. Did you write the fail condition before you ran anything? And could that

49
00:03:06,663 --> 00:03:09,988
result really happen? If you can't name one, it isn't a check yet.

50
00:03:10,058 --> 00:03:14,871
A Test That Can't Fail Isn't a Test. At Nik Bear Brown.
